{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My lesson shall drop as the rain.</b> An expression of flowing and descending, as in (Deuteronomy 33:28) ״יערפו טל״ — that is, it descended like rain according to its measure. Moses prays here that his words be heard and fixed in the hearts of the listeners. And the sense of לקחי is 'what I have taken and received from on high'; he entreats through them that his words produce an effect upon them like the effect the rain produces upon the earth — benefiting it, bringing it to birth, and causing it to sprout. And the phrase כשעירים רוחות is from the matter of רוח סערה (a stormwind), with the interchange of סמ\"ך and שי\"ן, as in the expression (Daniel 11) ״וישתער עליו״ — and this is the opinion of Onkelos, who translated 'כרוחי מטרא מנשבין על דתאה' — like winds of rain blowing upon grass, for the winds strengthen the grasses." "<b>Or,</b> one may interpret: כשעירים — the fine rain, upon דשא — is that which begins to sprout; and כרביבים — the great rain, upon עשב — these are the grasses whose growth is great. And כשעירים would then be as though with a צד\"י, from the word צעיר (young), similar to (Deuteronomy 33:19) ״ושפוני טמוני חול״, which is like וצפוני with a צד\"י." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> יערוף כמטר תזל כטל — just as rain is life for the world, so too are the words of Torah; and just as all rejoice in the dew, so too do they rejoice in the words of Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:02.383609172Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}